# ttychatter

Terminal-first AI chat clients for users who want a robust text-mode interface instead of a browser UI.

The project is now OpenRouter-first. OpenRouter provides the model catalog and chat-completions endpoint used by the current clients.

## Current flat layout

```text
ttychatter.ncurses
ttychatter.ncurses.1
ttychatter.ncurses.changelog

ttychatter.python
ttychatter.python.1
ttychatter.python.changelog

ttychatter.bash
ttychatter.bash.1
ttychatter.bash.changelog

docs/
c/
```

## Clients

### ttychatter.ncurses

Python ncurses full-screen client. This is the current reference implementation.

### ttychatter.python

Bash client with Python 3 helper snippets for robust JSON, model-cache parsing, attachment handling, and log/context maintenance.

### ttychatter.bash

Dependency-light Bash-only client. It avoids Python and uses jq when available. Without jq, JSON handling is best-effort but the program remains an end-user interactive client rather than a curl example.

## OpenRouter API key

Preferred environment variable:

```sh
export OPENROUTER_API_KEY="your_key"
```

Provider-neutral fallback:

```sh
export TTYCHATTER_API_KEY="your_key"
```

Config file key:

```text
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=your_key
```

## Config paths

Default config:

```text
~/.config/ttychatter/openrouter/config
```

Default sessions:

```text
~/.local/share/ttychatter/openrouter/sessions/
```

Default attachments:

```text
~/.local/share/ttychatter/openrouter/attachments/
```

Default model cache:

```text
~/.cache/ttychatter/openrouter/models.json
```

## Model catalog

OpenRouter model catalog:

```sh
curl https://openrouter.ai/api/v1/models \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $OPENROUTER_API_KEY"
```

Model IDs include fixed models and router-like selectors such as `openrouter/auto` and `openrouter/free`.

## Chat completion

OpenRouter chat-completions endpoint:

```text
https://openrouter.ai/api/v1/chat/completions
```

## Maintainer philosophy

The clients should remain user-facing terminal applications, not developer-only API examples. Keep comments verbose. Explain terminal behavior, JSON compromises, config compatibility, and provider assumptions so future maintainers can fork, repair, or port the project without guessing the original intent.
